⭐ SLIDE 3 — Semiconductor Yield Behaves Like an S-Curve

Key Observations
	•	Initial ramp is slow due to process immaturity
	•	Mid-phase rapid improvement from learning/actions
	•	Late-phase saturation as defects asymptotically reduce

Canonical Model
Y(t) = \frac{L}{1 + e^{-k(t - t_0)}}

Why Logistic?
	•	Arises from classic defect-density decay
	•	Matches physical manufacturing learning curve
	•	Smooth, monotonic, interpretable

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 4 — Why Weighted S-Curve?

Yield variance depends on testing volume

\text{Var}(Y) = \frac{p(1-p)}{n}

Where
	•	p = true yield
	•	n = number of dice (≈ volume)

So:

w_i = n_i

Meaning:
	•	High-volume weeks are more reliable
	•	Low-volume NPI weeks are noisy
	•	Weighting stabilizes the curve

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 5 — Mathematical Form of Weighted Fit

\min_{L,k,t_0} \sum_i n_i \cdot \left( Y_i - \frac{L}{1 + e^{-k(t_i - t_0)}} \right)^2

Interpretation
	•	Weighted nonlinear regression
	•	Statistically optimal under binomial noise
	•	Preserves physically meaningful S-shape
	•	Produces stable asymptote estimation

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 6 — Why Logistic Alone Is Not Enough

Real Micron products show systematic deviations due to:
	•	Fab-level offsets (tools, chambers, recipe changes)
	•	WaferGrade behavior differences
	•	DieGrade ramp shifts
	•	Category differences (Compute vs LP vs Graphics)
	•	Excursions and test-induced shifts

A single logistic cannot capture these second-order effects.

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 7 — Residual Learner Intuition

We decompose actual yield:

Y_{\text{actual}}(t) = Y_{\text{logistic}}(t) + \epsilon(t)

Where:
	•	Logistic = global physics of ramp
	•	Residual = system effects not modeled by logistic

Residual patterns come from:
	•	Category (Compute ramps faster, LP slower)
	•	WG/DG (grading rules influence ramp)
	•	Fab signature
	•	Volume instability
	•	Assembly/test impacts

Residuals are smooth, structured, and predictable → ideal for ML.

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 8 — ML Residual Model

Model: Random Forest Regressor
Predicts:
\hat{\epsilon}(t) = f_{\theta}(\text{NormWeek}, \text{Volume}, \text{Fab}, \text{WG}, \text{DG}, \text{Category})

Advantages:
	•	Learns nonlinear corrections
	•	Captures category-specific ramp shapes
	•	Captures fab/test systematic shifts
	•	Prevents ML from overriding global S-curve behavior

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 9 — Why Not Use ML Alone?

ML-only model issues:
	•	Produces non-monotonic, non-physical curves
	•	Overfits noise in low-volume weeks
	•	Cannot extrapolate NPI ramps
	•	Cannot guarantee asymptote or ramp structure

Thus ML should only model residuals, not base trend.

⸻

⭐ SLIDE 10 — Combined Model Architecture

\boxed{
Y_{\text{final}}(t)
=
Y_{\text{logistic}}(t)
+
\hat{\epsilon}(t)
}

Components
	•	Weighted S-curve = backbone
	•	Residual ML = correction layer

Benefits
	•	Interpretable
	•	Stable
	•	Captures real Micron behavior
	•	Accurate for PGM & NPI designs

